On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 3:52 PM, John E Clifford <kali9putra@yahoo.com>wrote:

> Sorry, to intrude philosophy (or theology) into this, but a case can be
> made that the only actual god has no worshipers, since all have worshiped
> imperfect simulcra of it -- and always will, even though they keep trying
> to get it right.  It is hard to worhsip the ineffable without effing a bit
> -- and thus missing.
>

And that is precisely why I don't have truck with philosophy. I see
absolutely no point in discussing things which can not be proven one way or
the other. God is unknowable in every aspect, number, gender, temperament,
existence. There is no way for us to find out anything about the true
nature of (the) god(s), and so there's no point that I can see in bothering
with trying. And like pretty much everything ever thought of by
philosophers, if and when we do figure out a way to make such things
knowable, we'll probably find out that all the philosophers were wrong.

Also, "the only actual god" sounds very Judeo-Christian. How could possibly
know whether lo cevni is singular? Maybe there's three, or 20, or a
million. The only thing we can say we know for certain is that we don't
know anything.


