> On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 1:22 PM, Alex Rozenshteyn <rpglover64@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> Well, it just means that if I wanted to talk about a god who existed
> whether or not ey was worshipped I'd need to use a zi'evla or {cevni be
> zi'o}. A priest might call the Christian god {lo cevni be zi'o bei roda}.
>
>
> Is there a such thing as a god which does not have worshipers? Granted,
> there are gods which do have worshipers /now/, but they did at one point,
> and I know of no gods that have never, at any time, had any worshipers at
> all.
>
>
> Even if you don't like philosophy, this is still a linguistic issue. In
> English, the Goa'uld are *not* gods, even though they are worshiped as
> gods; in lojban (assuming your definition as canonical, which makes sense)
> they *are* gods *because* they are worshiped as such.*
>
>
> *... to the people who worship them.
>
> On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 12:41 PM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> Meh. I don't have any truck with philosophers in general.
>
>
> On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 9:18 AM, Alex Rozenshteyn <rpglover64@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> Seems like this would interact nicely with the setting of *American Gods*,
> where {lo cevni be noda} are literally dying mostly because {noda ra cevni}.
>
> I think some theist philosophers would disagree with your definition,
> though; it's, in a sense, very anthropocentric; {pu ji'a sai lo nu zasti lo
> remna ku la .jegvon. ze'e ca cevni fi ro da}
>
>
> On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 5:32 AM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> On Sun, Dec 30, 2012 at 9:42 PM, Alex Rozenshteyn <rpglover64@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> So it seems like your notion of {cevni} focuses more centrally on worship
> and dominion than on divinity.
>
>
> No, it focus entirely on status. Their servants thought they were gods, we
> know they aren't. If someone think that X is god, then to that person, {da
> cevni}. If someone else does not think X is a god, or alternatively /knows/
> that X isn't, hen to /that/ person, {da na cevni}.
>
>
> On Sun, Dec 30, 2012 at 11:20 PM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> On Sun, Dec 30, 2012 at 8:24 PM, .arpis. <rpglover64+jbobau@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> Which is why I say misleading and not incorrect.
>
> Also, {la'oi Goa'uld cevni xu} by your definition (ignore if you haven't
> seen Stargate SG-1)?
>
>
> {.i go'i jenai go'i .i na cevni mi to fangypre po'o toi .i cevni lo gy.
> remna selfu}
> Yes and no. Not gods to me (just aliens), but gods to their human servants.
>
>
>  On Sun, Dec 30, 2012 at 9:17 PM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> On Sun, Dec 30, 2012 at 7:00 PM, .arpis. <rpglover64+jbobau@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> Then I believe that the jbovlaste definition of {cevni} is misleading:
>
>   Definition: x1 is a/the god/deity of people(s)/religion x2 with dominion
>        over x3 [sphere]; x1 is divine.
>
> note the secondary definition. I would consider angels to be divine.
>
>
> Well, then you're using a different sense of the word "divine" than
> jbovlaste is, because the sense of "divine" in the English definition of
> {cevni} is "(of or pertaining to a god): deific, godlike, godly", whereas
> you seem to be meaning "(eternal, holy): hallowed, holy, sacred".
>
> On Sun, Dec 30, 2012 at 8:38 PM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Even demons, and if you stretch the meaning /really/ far, djinn.
>
>
> On Sun, Dec 30, 2012 at 6:38 PM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> On Sun, Dec 30, 2012 at 1:28 PM, .arpis. <rpglover64+jbobau@gmail.com>wrote:
>
> xu se cumki smuni lo ka ce'u ge angeli gi cevni
>
>
> {cevni} can refer to many different things, but not angels. Gods,
> goddesses, demi-god(esse)s, oni, and so on and so forth.
>
> But not angels. Angels are not gods, they aren't even god-like. Except for
> maybe the archangels, they're barely above humans, and in many ways are
> lower on the totem pole (like the complete lack of free will, emotion, or
> creativity, for example).
